Identify the historical context and key figures associated with the introduction of the civil services system in India, including the relevant colonial and post-colonial developments.
The historical context of the introduction of the civil services system in India is rooted in British colonial rule. The British introduced a structured civil services system in India to administer their empire efficiently. This system was heavily influenced by the principles of meritocracy and bureaucratic organization, which were central to the British administrative model.

Key figures associated with this development include Lord Cornwallis, who played a significant role in establishing the Indian Civil Service (ICS) in 1784 through the Regulating Act. The ICS became a cornerstone of British governance in India, and it was characterized by its rigorous examination process and emphasis on loyalty to the British Crown.

Post-colonial developments saw the Indian government restructure the civil services system after independence in 1947. The Government of India Act of 1935 laid the foundation for a more representative and inclusive civil service, leading to the establishment of the All India Services, including the Indian Administrative Service (IAS), Indian Police Service (IPS), and Indian Forest Service (IFoS). These services were designed to serve the newly independent nation and were modeled on the British system but adapted to meet the needs of an independent India.
