Examine the specific policies, acts, or reforms that led to the formal establishment of the Indian Civil Services (ICS) and its evolution into the modern All India Services.
The formal establishment of the Indian Civil Services (ICS) can be traced back to the **Government of India Act, 1919**, which divided the Imperial Services into two categories: the All India Services and the Central Services. The ICS was one of the ten All India Services under this system.

Following the partition of British India in 1947, the ICS was split between the newly formed dominions of India and Pakistan. The Indian remnant of the ICS was reorganized and renamed the **Indian Administrative Service (IAS)**, while the Pakistani remnant became the **Pakistan Administrative Service (PAS)**.

The evolution of the ICS into the modern All India Services was further solidified by the **All India Services Act, 1951**. This act, under **Article 312(2) of the Constitution of India**, established two All India Services: the **Indian Administrative Service (IAS)** and the **Indian Police Service (IPS)**. These services were created based on the legacy of the ICS and the Imperial Police, respectively, and marked the formal institutionalization of the modern All India Services system in independent India.
