Determine the key legislative or constitutional provisions that led to the formation of the original PSC and its role in civil services recruitment.
The key legislative or constitutional provision that led to the formation of the original Public Service Commission (PSC) is the White Paper Colonial 197 issued in 1950. This document proposed measures to improve the quality and efficiency of the Colonial Service under British administration. Specifically, paragraph 21(xi) of the White Paper suggested the establishment of public service commissions. 

The role of the PSC in civil services recruitment involves regulating the employment and working conditions of civil servants, overseeing hiring and promotions, and promoting the values of the public service. It also conducts examinations for recruitment to all India services and higher Central services, as outlined in Articles 315 to 323 of the Constitution of India.
