Explain the role of tight junctions in maintaining cellular barriers and regulating paracellular transport across these locations
Tight junctions play a crucial role in maintaining cellular barriers by forming a seal between adjacent cells in epithelial layers. They act as a paracellular barrier that prevents the leakage of transported solutes and water, sealing the paracellular pathway. Tight junctions regulate paracellular transport by controlling the passage of small ions and water-soluble solutes through the paracellular space while preventing the passage of larger molecules such as luminal antigens and microorganisms. This selective permeability allows tight junctions to adapt to different physiological and pathological conditions, ensuring the proper function of epithelial tissues.
