Identify the historical context of the Mona Lisa's location prior to World War II and the reasons for its relocation.
Prior to World War II, the Mona Lisa was located at the Louvre in Paris. During the Franco-Prussian War (1870–71), it was temporarily moved to the Brest Arsenal for safety. The painting remained at the Louvre until the outbreak of World War II, when it was again relocated to protect it from potential damage or theft due to the war's chaos. It was first moved to Château d'Amboise, then to the Loc-Dieu Abbey and Château de Chambord, and finally to the Ingres Museum in Montauban.
