Provide context on the structure of the Supreme Court, including its composition and historical background.
The Supreme Court of the United States is the highest-ranking judicial body in the nation, established by Article III of the Constitution. The detailed structure of the Court was initially laid down by the 1st United States Congress in 1789, which specified the Court's original and appellate jurisdiction, created 13 judicial districts, and set the initial size of the Supreme Court. The number of justices on the Court changed several times before it settled at nine in 1869. This number was solidified by the Judiciary Act of 1869, which prevented the elimination of the eighth seat following the death of Justice James Moore Wayne in 1867 and added an additional seat to make the total nine. 

The composition of the Court includes one Chief Justice and eight associate justices. Justices are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate. Once appointed, they serve for life unless they resign, retire, or are removed from office. Each justice has a single vote in deciding cases, with the Chief Justice's vote being no more than that of any other justice. When in the majority, the Chief Justice decides who writes the court's opinion; otherwise, the senior justice in the majority assigns the writing of a decision.
