Identify the anatomical structure of a plant leaf and explain the location and function of the mesophyll within it
The anatomical structure of a plant leaf includes the lamina (the flat, expanded part of the leaf), the petiole (which connects the leaf to the stem), and the vascular system. The mesophyll is a key tissue within the leaf, located between the upper and lower epidermis. It consists of two main layers: the palisade mesophyll and the spongy mesophyll. The palisade mesophyll, which is primarily responsible for photosynthesis, is typically found on the upper side of the leaf blade. In some species, such as mature Eucalyptus leaves, the palisade mesophyll may be present on both sides of the leaf. The mesophyll plays a crucial role in photosynthesis and also facilitates the transport of water and nutrients between the leaf and the stem.
