Describe the composition of the mesophyll, including its cellular structures and their roles in photosynthesis
The mesophyll is the inner tissue of a leaf, primarily composed of parenchyma cells. These cells are crucial for photosynthesis and contain numerous chloroplasts, which are responsible for capturing light energy. The mesophyll cells are specialized for this function, with some cells containing up to 450,000 to 800,000 chloroplasts per square millimeter of leaf. Chloroplasts contain pigments such as chlorophyll, which are arranged in antenna proteins to work together in light-harvesting complexes. This arrangement allows the mesophyll to efficiently absorb light and convert it into chemical energy through the process of photosynthesis. Additionally, the mesophyll plays a role in gas exchange, facilitating the intake of carbon dioxide and the release of oxygen.
