Identify the key events in Michael Jordan's basketball career that led to his final retirement, including his playing years, major milestones, and official retirement announcement.
Michael Jordan's basketball career included several key events that led to his final retirement. He played professionally from 1984 to 2003. His major milestones include winning six NBA championships with the Chicago Bulls, earning five NBA Most Valuable Player awards, and being named the greatest basketball player of the 20th century by the Associated Press. Jordan's athletic leaping ability and back-to-back Slam Dunk Contest championships in 1987 and 1988 influenced a generation of players. 

Jordan officially retired from basketball for the first time on January 13, 1999, as mentioned in the film "Michael Jordan: An American Hero." However, he made a short-lived comeback in 2001 when he joined the Washington Wizards, playing until 2003 when he retired again. His final retirement was announced in 2003, marking the end of his professional basketball career.
