Identify the earliest known historical and linguistic usage of the word 'catholic' in its religious context, including its etymology and early theological applications
The earliest known historical and linguistic usage of the word "catholic" in its religious context can be traced back to early Christian writings. The term originates from the Greek word "katholikos," which means "universal" or "according to the whole." This etymology reflects the early Church's emphasis on the universality of its faith and practices.

In theological applications, the term was used to describe the universal nature of the Christian faith, encompassing all believers and emphasizing the unity of the Church. It was employed in early Christian texts to denote the comprehensive and inclusive character of the Church's teachings and practices, distinguishing it from particular or localized expressions of Christianity. This usage is evident in writings from the 1st and 2nd centuries, such as those by Ignatius of Antioch and Justin Martyr, who spoke of the "Catholic Church" as a universal community of believers.
