Determine which form of iron has the highest absorption rate in the human body and explain why
The form of iron with the highest absorption rate in the human body is heme iron. This is because heme iron, found in animal products such as meat, blood, and heme-containing proteins, has a significantly higher bioavailability compared to non-heme iron. Specifically, heme iron has an absorption rate of 15-35%, whereas non-heme iron typically has an absorption rate of only 2-20%. The higher absorption rate of heme iron is due to its structure, which allows it to be more efficiently absorbed in the duodenum by enterocytes. Additionally, heme iron is less affected by dietary factors that inhibit iron absorption, making it a more reliable source of dietary iron.
