Explain the function and destination of the pulmonary artery in the circulatory system
The pulmonary artery is a blood vessel in the circulatory system that carries deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs. Its primary function is to transport this blood to the lungs, where gas exchange occurs. In the lungs, carbon dioxide is released from the blood, and oxygen is absorbed into the bloodstream. After this exchange, the now oxygen-rich blood is returned to the heart via the pulmonary veins, completing the pulmonary circulation cycle.
