Detail the pathway of blood flow from the pulmonary artery to the lungs and its subsequent return to the heart
The pathway of blood flow begins in the pulmonary artery, where deoxygenated blood is carried from the right ventricle of the heart to the lungs. Once in the lungs, the blood travels through smaller branches of the pulmonary artery, eventually reaching the capillaries that surround the pulmonary alveoli. In the alveoli, gas exchange occurs, and the blood becomes oxygenated. The oxygenated blood then flows into the pulmonary veins, which transport it back to the left atrium of the heart, completing the cycle.
