Trace the historical origins and evolution of the French national anthem, including its creation, adoption, and any significant changes over time.
The French national anthem, "La Marseillaise," was created in 1792 during the French Revolution. It was initially adopted as the anthem of the French Republic in 1795. During Napoleon I's reign, "Veillons au salut de l'Empire" served as the unofficial anthem, and under Napoleon III, "Partant pour la Syrie" was used. 

"La Marseillaise" gained recognition as the anthem of the international revolutionary movement and was adopted by the Paris Commune in 1871 with new lyrics under the title "La Marseillaise de la Commune." It was officially restored as France's national anthem in 1879 and has remained so ever since. The anthem has its roots in revolutionary fervor and has been associated with various historical movements in France.
