Identify the architectural style of the Great Mosque of Córdoba and explain its defining characteristics, including its historical influences and structural features.
The Great Mosque of Córdoba was built in the Umayyad style of architecture, with variations inspired by indigenous Roman and Christian Visigothic structures. Its defining characteristics include a hypostyle hall with 856 columns made from materials such as jasper, onyx, marble, granite, and porphyry, which were sourced from Roman temples and other buildings. The mosque is notable for its double arches, which allowed for higher ceilings than traditional designs. Additionally, it featured intricate mosaics, domed bays, and arches. The architectural layout and style of the Great Mosque of Córdoba share similarities with the Great Mosque of Damascus, suggesting influences from the ancestral lands of the Arabs in Syria.
