Explain the free care requirement under Hill-Burton and identify its intended purpose in the context of healthcare access and public health policy
The Hill-Burton Act, enacted in 1946, mandated that hospitals receiving federal funds for construction or modernization must provide a certain amount of free or reduced-cost care to patients who cannot afford to pay. The intended purpose of this requirement was to improve healthcare access for low-income individuals and underserved populations, thereby promoting public health and reducing disparities in healthcare access. This provision aimed to ensure that hospitals served as community resources, offering essential services to those in need, which aligned with broader public health policy goals of equity and accessibility in healthcare.
