The political and administrative changes in Hawaii leading up to Honolulu's designation as the capital were influenced by the movement of the Hawaiian royal family. The royal family relocated from Hawaii Island to Maui, and then to Oahu, which contributed to the modern-day distribution of population centers. Kamehameha III selected Honolulu as his capital due to its natural harbor, the present-day Honolulu Harbor. This decision ultimately led to Honolulu becoming the state capital.
