Identify the origin and historical context of daylight saving time, including key events and countries involved in its adoption.
Daylight saving time (DST) originated in the late 19th century, with the idea first proposed by George Hudson in 1895. The first nationwide implementation was organized by the German Empire and Austria-Hungary, starting on April 30, 1916. This initiative aimed to conserve energy by extending daylight hours during the summer months. Following this, many countries adopted DST at various times, particularly after the energy crisis of the 1970s, which prompted further enactments and adjustments. The practice involves advancing clocks forward one hour in spring and setting them back in autumn to standard time.
