Identify the primary technique used to measure the electrical activity of brain cells directly, including its method of operation and key characteristics
The primary technique used to measure the electrical activity of brain cells directly is the electroencephalogram (EEG). This method involves placing electrical leads (electrodes) all over the scalp to measure the collective electrical activity of the cerebral cortex. EEG specifically measures the electric fields generated by large groups of neurons, providing excellent temporal resolution and the ability to capture aggregate activity down to the millisecond scale. However, it has limited spatial resolution, making it less effective at distinguishing activity among different groups of neurons compared to other imaging methods.
