Explain the technical principles and applications of the identified technique in neuroscience research
The technical principles and applications of brain mapping in neuroscience research involve the study of the anatomy and function of the brain and spinal cord using a variety of advanced techniques. These include imaging methods such as intra-operative, microscopic, endoscopic, and multi-modality imaging, immunohistochemistry, molecular and optogenetics, stem cell and cellular biology, engineering (material, electrical, and biomedical), neurophysiology, and nanotechnology. Brain mapping aims to map biological quantities or properties onto spatial representations of the brain, providing insights into neural structures and functions. This technique is used to understand complex processes such as learning, memory, behavior, perception, and consciousness, and it facilitates interdisciplinary research by integrating findings from neuroscience, psychology, and other related fields.
