Explain the physical mechanism by which these waves transfer energy through a medium
Mechanical waves transfer energy through a medium by the oscillation of matter. When a mechanical wave is generated, it requires an initial energy input, which causes particles in the medium to vibrate around their equilibrium positions. These vibrations are passed from one particle to the next, allowing the wave to propagate through the medium. Although the particles themselves do not move far from their original positions, the energy carried by the wave moves through the medium in the same direction as the wave. This process relies on the medium's elasticity and inertia, as the restoring forces within the medium reverse the deformation caused by the wave, facilitating the continuous transfer of energy.
