Identify the primary gas released during land conversion through biomass burning and explain the chemical processes involved in its emission
The primary gas released during land conversion through biomass burning is methane (CH₄). 

During biomass burning, organic matter undergoes thermal decomposition when exposed to high temperatures. This process, known as pyrolysis, involves the breaking of chemical bonds in the organic material, leading to the release of various gases, including methane. Methane is produced primarily from the incomplete combustion of carbon-based materials. Incomplete burning occurs when there is insufficient oxygen available for complete oxidation, resulting in the formation of methane rather than carbon dioxide. Additionally, the decomposition of organic matter under anaerobic conditions can also produce methane as a byproduct. Thus, methane emissions are significant during biomass burning due to both combustion and decomposition processes.
