The Springfield Model 1861 was a Minié-type rifled musket used by the United States Army and Marine Corps during the American Civil War. It became the most widely used U.S. Army weapon during the conflict, favored for its range, accuracy, and reliability. The rifle was primarily used by Union infantry, with its adoption being gradual across different theaters of the war. Western armies were slower to adopt the Springfield rifle compared to those in the eastern theater.
