Explain the role and responsibilities of the Rajya Sabha's chairperson within the Indian parliamentary system
The Rajya Sabha's chairperson, known as the Vice-President of India, holds the most senior position in the country after the President. Their primary role is to function as the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha. This includes presiding over the sessions of the Council of States, ensuring the effective functioning of the Sabha, and maintaining order during debates. The Vice-President also plays a ceremonial role, with no real authority vested in the office, except for their duties as Chairman of the Rajya Sabha. In the event of a vacancy in the President's office, the Vice-President assumes the role of Acting President until a new president is elected.
