Determine the specific individual or individuals entombed within the Great Pyramid of Giza based on archaeological evidence and historical records
Based on archaeological evidence and historical records, the Great Pyramid of Giza (also known as the Pyramid of Khufu) is believed to have originally been intended as a tomb for Pharaoh Khufu, who ruled during the Fourth Dynasty of ancient Egypt. However, the interior of the pyramid was found to be empty when it was first explored. The burial chamber, which was originally sealed, did not contain the expected mummy or sarcophagus. 

While the pyramid itself does not contain the remains of Khufu, there is archaeological evidence suggesting that the pyramid complex includes other tombs. Specifically, the tomb of Queen Hetepheres I, sister-wife of Sneferu and mother of Khufu, was discovered beneath the paving around the Great Pyramid. This tomb, found by the Reisner expedition, was intact but contained an empty coffin.

Thus, based on archaeological findings, the specific individual entombed within the Great Pyramid of Giza is not definitively known, but the tomb of Queen Hetepheres I is located within the Giza pyramid complex.
