Identify the historical origin and initial implementation of daylight saving time in the United States, including key legislative actions and the first official start date.
Daylight saving time (DST) in the United States was first implemented on March 31, 1918, through the Daylight Saving Act of 1917. This act was enacted by the Dominion of Newfoundland, making it one of the first jurisdictions in North America to adopt DST. The United States officially began observing DST on March 31, 1918, as part of the Standard Time Act of 1918, also known as the Calder Act. This legislation established time zones and introduced DST, although it was later repealed in 1919. DST was re-established nationally early in World War II and has been continuously observed since then, with further standardization occurring in 1966.
