Determine the approximate time period when this separation occurred, including any relevant geological or climatic factors
The separation of Australia from Antarctica began during the Permian period and continued through to the Cretaceous, approximately 84 million years ago (Ma). This process was influenced by the breakup of the supercontinent Gondwana. As Gondwana moved northward, the eastern margin warmed, and tectonic activity led to the rifting between Australia and Antarctica. The separation was part of a broader plate tectonic process that also involved the splitting of other landmasses such as India and New Zealand. The climatic factors included the warming of the region as it moved toward the equator, which contributed to the geological changes and the eventual separation of these landmasses.
