Identify the historical event and its key characteristics related to the forced relocation of the Cherokee Nation
The historical event is the Trail of Tears, which refers to the forced relocation of the Cherokee Nation from their ancestral homelands in the Southeastern United States to the Indian Territory (present-day Oklahoma) between 1836 and 1839. Key characteristics include the forced march of over 16,000 Cherokee people, the use of government authorities following the Indian Removal Act of 1830, and the significant human toll, with an estimated 4,000 Cherokee deaths due to disease, exposure, and starvation during the journey. The event is also known as "Nu na da ul tsun yi" (the place where they cried) or "Tlo va sa" (our removal) in Cherokee.
