Identify the historical context of the Abbasid Caliphate and determine which city was established as its new capital, including the reasons for its selection.
The Abbasid Caliphate was established in the 8th century following the Abbasid Revolution of 750 CE, which led to the overthrow of the Umayyad Caliphate. The new caliphate was founded by a dynasty descended from Muhammad's uncle, Abbas ibn Abdul-Muttalib. Initially centered in Kufa, modern-day Iraq, the Abbasids later moved their capital to Baghdad in 762 CE under the rule of Caliph Al-Mansur. 

Baghdad was chosen as the new capital due to its strategic location at the confluence of major trade routes, which facilitated economic and cultural exchange. Additionally, the city was envisioned as a center of learning and governance, reflecting the Abbasid emphasis on knowledge and intellectual pursuits. This decision contributed to the city becoming a significant hub for science, philosophy, medicine, and education, exemplified by the establishment of the House of Wisdom.
