Identify the historical context and key figures involved in the establishment of Jamestown, including the primary sponsors, the Virginia Company, and the leadership structure of the colony.
The historical context of Jamestown involves the establishment of the first permanent English settlement in the New World on May 13, 1607. The primary sponsors of the colony were the Virginia Company, a joint-stock company formed by English investors to finance the expedition and settlement. The leadership structure of the colony was initially governed by a council of investors and settlers, with Edward Maria Wingfield serving as the first elected president of the colony. Wingfield played a significant role in the early governance and construction of James Fort, which was described by John Smith as "a very great place for the erecting of a great city." The Virginia Company's records provide insight into the colony's early history, although some documents have been lost over time.
