Determine the specific individual or group responsible for founding Jamestown, including the role of Captain John Smith and the significance of the first permanent English settlement in North America.
The specific individual or group responsible for founding Jamestown was the Virginia Company, a joint-stock company chartered by King James I of England in 1606. The Virginia Company organized the first permanent English settlement in North America, which was established in 1607 at Jamestown, Virginia. Captain John Smith played a significant role in the early years of the colony, serving as a leader of the Virginia Colony based at Jamestown between September 1608 and August 1609. He led explorations along the rivers of Virginia and the Chesapeake Bay, becoming the first English explorer to map the Chesapeake Bay area. His leadership and exploration efforts were crucial in establishing the colony's survival and development. The significance of Jamestown lies in it being the first permanent English settlement in North America, marking the beginning of English colonization in the continent.
