Examine the historical context and key figures associated with the development of the first hydrogen fuel cell
The historical context of the development of the first hydrogen fuel cell begins in 1838 when Welsh physicist and barrister William Grove wrote about his initial crude fuel cells in a letter dated October 1838, published in the December 1838 edition of "The London and Edinburgh Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science." Grove's work laid the foundation for the concept of fuel cells. The principle of a fuel cell was also discovered independently by Christian Friedrich Schönbein in 1838. In 1839, Sir William Robert Grove constructed the first fuel cell, which was most similar to today's phosphoric acid fuel cells. These early developments marked the beginning of fuel cell technology.
