Identify the specific hurricane Edith in 1971 and determine its path and landfall location
Hurricane Edith in 1971 was the strongest hurricane of that Atlantic hurricane season and the southernmost Category 5 hurricane on record in the Atlantic until surpassed by Hurricane Felix in 2007. It developed from a tropical wave on September 5 and quickly strengthened into a hurricane in the Caribbean Sea. On September 9, it rapidly intensified and made landfall on Cape Gracias a Dios as a Category 5 hurricane. After crossing Central America, it re-emerged into the Gulf of Mexico near Campeche, Mexico on September 11 as a minimal tropical storm. It then moved northwestward, making landfall near Belize City with tropical storm winds of 70 mph (115 km/h). Continuing its path, it eventually turned to the east-northeast, approached the coast of Louisiana, and made landfall on September 16 in a sparsely populated area 30 miles (48 km) east of Cameron with winds of 105 mph (170 km/h), equivalent to a Category 2 hurricane. The storm continued to weaken over land, dissipating over northwestern Georgia on September 18.
