Determine the exact date and circumstances of the last POW repatriation event, including any official records or documentation that confirm this date.
The last POW repatriation event during the Vietnam War was part of **Operation Homecoming**, which took place from **February through April 1973**. This operation involved the return of U.S. prisoners of war (POWs) to the United States following the **Paris Peace Accords** signed in January 1973. 

Official records confirm that the final POW repatriation under this operation occurred on **April 29, 1973**, when the last group of American POWs was released from North Vietnam and returned to the U.S. These events are documented by the **United States Senate Select Committee on POW/MIA Affairs**, which investigated the fate of missing-in-action personnel during the Vietnam War. The committee's work, conducted during the George H. W. Bush administration (1989–1993), included reviewing historical records and testimonies related to the repatriation process.
